{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For the soul of the flesh is in the blood, and I have given it to you upon the altar.</b> The Ramban, zatzal, wrote many reasons for the prohibition of blood. One: because it is the portion of HaShem like the fat, for just as the fat is offered up, as it is written (Leviticus 3:2) ״כל חלב לה׳״ — 'all fat belongs to HaShem' — so the blood is dashed upon the altar, and therefore the Torah forbade the fat and the blood because they are His portion, may He be exalted. And as for the fact that the blood of a wild animal is forbidden, though it is not the portion of HaShem since it is not fit for an offering — this is because one does not enact a prohibition for halves, and therefore Scripture wished to forbid all blood, whether of domesticated animal or of wild animal. Another reason: because it is the soul, and the Holy One, blessed be He, did not permit the soul in the world. For we find in the case of the first man that all living creatures were forbidden to him, and he was permitted to eat nothing but herbage and fruits, until Noah came and the Holy One, blessed be He, permitted him all living creatures, because they were saved only in his merit. And what He permitted to him in them was specifically the body, not the soul; if so, the soul remains under its original prohibition. Another reason: because the blood is the bestial soul, it is not fitting for us that we should mix its nature with our nature, and we who receive the Torah must be such that our bodies are pure, fit to receive the intelligibles. And we were commanded to elevate our nature to be soft and merciful, not cruel; and if we were to eat the blood, it would engender in our souls cruelty and coarseness of nature, so that it would approximate the bestial soul — for the eaten thing becomes flesh in the body of the eater and produces in him a nature like its own. And blood is not like other foods that are transformed in digestion; therefore it says ״כי נפש הבשר בדם היא״ — 'for the soul of the flesh is in the blood' — for all flesh has its soul in the blood, and it is not fitting for us to mix the severed soul with the existing soul, but rather it should serve for atonement upon the altar. All these reasons are from our rabbis, zatzal." "<b>And the Rambam,</b> zatzal, wrote in the reasons for the commandments regarding the prohibition of blood that it is a distancing, so that a person should not engage in the practice of shedim (demons). For when Israel went out of Egypt, they were expert in this lore and habituated to it from the Egyptians, who were steeped in and drawn after it. And the wise men among them, when they wished to assemble them, would dig a pit and cast the blood there, and the shedim would gather around the pit; and when they wished to divine the future, they would eat of the blood. And this is what Scripture warned (Leviticus 19): ״לא תאכלו על הדם״ — 'you shall not eat upon the blood'; (1 Samuel 14): ״הנה העם חוטאים לה׳ לאכול על הדם״ — 'the people are sinning against HaShem, eating upon the blood.' And because of this the Torah forbade the eating of blood, for the Torah's intention is always to demolish the structures of their folly by inverting their thoughts. And this is the reason for its prohibition: Scripture forbade it in eating and chose it for purification by sprinkling and for dashing upon the altar for atonement. And since this matter is a species of idolatry, Scripture mentioned concerning it ״ונתתי פני בנפש האוכלת את הדם״ — 'I will set My face against the soul that eats the blood' — just as it mentioned concerning one who gives of his seed to Molech. This is the opinion of the Rav, zatzal, in the Guide of the Perplexed." "<b>And the aforementioned Rav</b> wrote concerning this that if this were the principal reason, why would Scripture mention ״נפש נפש״ — 'soul, soul' — each and every time it mentions the prohibition of blood? And therefore he wrote that the matter sits well upon the heart, but the language of Scripture does not indicate thus, and consequently he explained the prohibition of blood with all the reasons that I wrote above. We further need to clarify in the verses of blood the following: it is written ״כי נפש הבשר בדם היא״ — 'for the soul of the flesh is in the blood' — and it is written ״כי נפש כל בשר דמו בנפשו הוא״ — 'for the soul of all flesh, its blood is in its soul' — and these two verses indicate that the soul is in the blood and the blood is in the soul, the two being intermingled together like wine mingled in water, where the water is in the wine and the wine in the water, each within the other. And afterward he explained and said: ״דם כל בשר לא תאכלו כי נפש כל בשר דמו היא״ — 'the blood of all flesh you shall not eat, for the soul of all flesh is its blood' — this comes to clarify that the blood is the soul itself, the two being as one flesh that cannot be separated; neither blood without soul nor soul without blood is found, like the relation of matter and form in all corporeal beings, where the one is never found without the other. And this blood, which is the soul, is called דם שותת — oozing blood — which seeps and cannot flow; and it is the blood that clots, and this is the last drop (revi'it acharon). But all the first blood, which is flowing blood, is called 'the blood of the soul,' but it is not the soul itself, for a person or animal does not die from it but rather from the last drop. But regarding the penalty of karet, it is unnecessary to specify oozing blood, for even flowing blood is subject to karet."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:25.078769067Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}